<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Tuttul</h1><p class="st-meta">2500 BCE – 1760 BCE (approx.) · capital Tuttul</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Tuttul, a city on the Euphrates near modern Raqqa, sacred to the god Dagan, where Sargon of Akkad prayed before conquering the upper Euphrates.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tuttul stood at Tell Bi'a where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balikh-river">Balikh</a> river meets the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, an ideal place for river and caravan traffic. It was a substantial city by the mid-3rd millennium BCE and became famous as the cult centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagan">Dagan</a>, the chief god of the middle Euphrates.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a> recorded that he bowed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagan">Dagan</a> at Tuttul, who granted him <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mari">Mari</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ebla">Ebla</a> and the cedar forests. In the 18th century BCE Tuttul was a seat of Amorite tribal kings who fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yahdun-lim">Yahdun-Lim</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>, and later fell under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamshi-adad-i">Shamshi-Adad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimri-lim">Zimri-Lim</a>. German excavations from 1980 uncovered palaces and tombs.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/babylonian-empire">Babylonian Empire</a> destroyed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mari">Mari</a>, Tuttul declined and lost its independence around 1760 BCE. It remained a minor settlement, and its sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagan">Dagan</a> faded from the record.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tuttul.jpg" alt="Tuttul" width="320" height="214"><figcaption>Image: Bertramz · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tuttul,tombs.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>2500 BCE – 1760 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/26th-century-bce">26th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century-bce">18th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tuttul</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bahlu-kulim</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Syrian Semitic religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Amorite, Akkadian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tell Bi'a</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-2130&amp;focus=tuttul" aria-label="Tuttul on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5665 879 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tuttul lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5985 1135L6039 1183"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6039 1183h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5985 1135h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5985 1135h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-2130&amp;focus=tuttul">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

